A||||31||the need for speed||slim head light conversion||||1||
Z||000000||the need for speed||09-30-2000||07:50 PM||||I like the look of rms slim head light conversion but i dont like the price $275 does any one know a way to make this or some thing similiar<BR> <A HREF="http://webhome.idirect.com/~rsm1/slim.jpg" TARGET=_blank>http://webhome.idirect.com/~rsm1/slim.jpg</A> <BR> <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/waycool_1.gif" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/waycool_1.gif</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000001||theogre||09-30-2000||09:09 PM||||I can tell you this. The RSM one is not street legal anywhere in the U.S. It uses fog lamps to replace the headlight.<P>The bottom one is described someplace. I for get where. (I read it recently too... nuts.) I recall it uses a DOT legal lamp. It's not a kit just something someone did.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 09-30-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||Sillhouette.com||09-30-2000||10:26 PM||||My how fast we are at jumping at RSM.  If it is not fuctionallity or reliability than it's not DOT approved.  Oh well to each to it's own.  <P>Talking from a direct point of view (I designed the thing)  You no longer have problems with the headlight doors popping open at high wind or speed conditions.  The headlight motors don't turn as long so more reliable.  I had the install for a year and a half drove it through Canadian winter without any snags.  It has a much better illumination capability than the stock lights.  Oh yeah you can use any light that you want.  <P>The only way you can have your lights DOT approved is if you use the stock lights.  Even if it states on the light itself that it's DOT approved it will not be in the Fiero application.  <P>So keep your frog lookalike headlights DOT approved and I'll zoom by you with a way better looking Fiero and see much better as well.  <P>Cheers||||reg||1
Z||000003||BOILERMAKER||09-30-2000||11:02 PM||||Wow!  Someone woke up on the wrong side of the keyboard.  I didn't take OGRE'S comment as a slam, but rather as an information insert.  Lighten up, I like them both.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Seanh||09-30-2000||11:04 PM||||I give up. This is the second time I have had to retype this message. First time I hit the Clear Fields button, second time I hit the damn back button. For the third time:<BR> <A HREF="http://members.nccw.net/webe/other/fiero/lowprofile.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://members.nccw.net/webe/other/fiero/lowprofile.htm</A>   This site tells how to do the conversion in the second pict. I am having it done with HID's. Probally dont want to use HIDs because the set ran me $920 bucks. In order to do this you will have to have or upgrade to the 87+ headlight controller system. Motors can get pretty Pricy. I will picture them when they are complete in February when I rebuild my car.<P>------------------<BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8<BR>Killer Stereo System||||reg||1
Z||000005||theogre||09-30-2000||11:38 PM||||Someone here said the gen 2 light motor parts are available off some other car. A Sunbird I think. I'd install gen 2 motors on any Fiero that doesn't have them rather than fix the old system when it breaks. The used gen 2 system likely costs less than fixing the gen 1 motors. (depends how bad off the old gen 1 motor is I guess.)<P>I wasn't making attacks on anyone. I don't know or care really who built either of the above conversions. It has nothing to do with how I think either the conversions or the original look. It has nothing to do with reliability of the result. <P>The point is that, as shown, the RSM one won't pass inspection in allot of places because of the lamping used. If the RSM one can use other lamps that's fine. It wasn't an issue of being fully legal. It's making it look like it belongs there. If an inspector in most mid atlantic and north east states sees those 2 fog type lamps they are very likey to fail you for having no headlights. Cops in allot of towns around here are likely to stop you for the same reason. (College towns are especially great for this type thing. They even do things like measure bumper hight and ground clearance.)<P>The web electric one done with HID might not pass inspecion in allot of places either. Inspectors and cops know HID wasn't available in the 80's. <P>Done the way Lawrence did it with a diferant size of standard headlamp is more likely not to be an inspection problem or a ticket magnet. The non stock factor means it may not be fully legal but it looks like an OE option to people who don't know any better. <P>There are all kinds of regs on lights on the front of cars in the U.S. They only start with DOT issues. Most states have other regs on top of that, like regulating the physical size, brighness, placement and number of auxilary lights. <P>I know people that have been written tickets for uncovered aux lights on truck roll bars that aren't even hooked up.<BR><p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 09-30-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||Sillhouette.com||10-01-2000||02:45 AM||||Why do you have to convert to an 87 headlight motor?  I just finished one for a 84 with it's standard headlight motor.  The other thing 920 dollars for a set of headlights.  Man that's a lot of money. I assume that you are talking about the type of lights that are on a high end BMW.  Those lights don't have filaments they have just two metals at close proximity that allow the electricty to jump accross like a welding torch.  Are you aware that you have to provide a lot of volts for that to work.  I'm interested in seeing how you come up with this.  I think the Fiero electrical system won't handle it. <P>Second thing how does you governing agencies (cops) decide on the brightness of a light when you got the BMW and Lexus High intensity lights as standard?  In your neck of woods these cars are not allowed?<P>||||reg||1
Z||000007||DaRkLoRD||10-01-2000||03:18 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Sillhouette.com:<BR><B>Why do you have to convert to an 87 headlight motor?  I just finished one for a 84 with it's standard headlight motor</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>How did you get the headlight doors to only open partway? I'm planning a low-profile headlight mod of my own, using Camaro lights like in Tom's kit.. I've heard the 87-88 headlight assemblies are easier to use for this kind of modification.<P>------------------<BR>  <A HREF="mailto:steve@fieroproject.com">steve@fieroproject.com</A>  <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroproject.com</A>||||reg||1
Z||000008||Seanh||10-01-2000||09:34 AM||||I had to convert to the 87+ system because they are controlled electronically. 86 and older systems are controlled with a timed relay system. One the 87+ systems the motors shut off when they read a stall current. I put a stop on the shaft so when they come up, once they hit the stop they read a stall current and shut the motors off. If you did this on and older system, you would ruin the motors quickly becauase they run for a set period of time and will not shut off once they reach your stop and will wear the motors out. My system will use HID's, the ones in High-end cars. I can understand why using fog lamps to replace headlights would be illegal, but why would it be illegal to do a conversion like mine or Lawances? Espacially mine, sice it uses a kit designed to replace a factory system if someone wanted HID's and it was not offered as an option. I know I will not have any trouble locally, but you have me kinda worried about highway driving and long trips. I can count on the cops being as stupid as they are here. Thanks<P>Oh yes, suppling power for the HID's. My car already has a 170A alt used to power my stereo system, this shoud be enought to power the ballasts for the HID's which inturn will provide the voltage they need.<P>------------------<BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8<BR>Killer Stereo System||||reg||1
Z||000009||Sillhouette.com||10-01-2000||10:00 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Seanh:<BR><B>I had to convert to the 87+ system because they are controlled electronically. 86 and older systems are controlled with a timed relay system. One the 87+ systems the motors shut off when they read a stall current. I put a stop on the shaft so when they come up, once they hit the stop they read a stall current and shut the motors off. If you did this on and older system, you would ruin the motors quickly becauase they run for a set period of time and will not shut off once they reach your stop and will wear the motors out. </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>IF that is true why is it that the old systems have a rubber bushing on both ends of travel for the motor?  If you got a time delay relay you don't need any stopps whatsoever. ||||reg||1
Z||000010||lawrence||10-01-2000||10:08 AM||||My headlights are not DOT approved.  In order to do that, I would have to apply to the government and give them samples for testing.<P>I used standard low beam headlights and replaced the inner bulb with a dual filament 9007.  Because I chose a low beam, the light pattern is correct.  This makes it functional for me, and safe for oncoming drivers.  I don't feel that I'm compromising anybody's safety.<P>Am I worried about the cops?  Nope.  They have better things to do than look at my headlights, especially since they are only visible at night anyway.  Even if one did, he would probably only look at the fixture and read DOT on the lens.<P>I don't see how this can be a big deal when they led SUVs go with less than EPA required mileage, just because they are not classified as cars, or because they let other regs go on cars with low production volumes.<P>Yeah.  That's it, I'll just claim low production B^)<P>-L<BR> <A HREF="http://www.webelectricmagazine.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.webelectricmagazine.com</A> <BR> <A HREF="http://members.nccw.net/webe/sts" TARGET=_blank>http://members.nccw.net/webe/sts</A>  - Sequentials<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000011||Seanh||10-01-2000||10:26 AM||||Yes, If you change the time delay, you would be fine using the 86- systems. I dont think I would have any trouble with the lights not being legal or anything. I am still going to do it. "Shoot officer, them things was on there when I got this here car, what ya callin em, hids?"<P>------------------<BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8<BR>Killer Stereo System||||reg||1
Z||000012||theogre||10-01-2000||11:25 AM||||Most SUV's are built/classed as trucks. trucks don't have the same regs as cars for allot of things.<P>I didn't say yours are fully legal lawrence. just that they look like they belong there. because of that they are less interesting to inspectors.<P>HID lights have a "ballast" that run them. Similar to setting up a flouresent lights or neon in a car. The ballast runs off 12v same as everything else. Just how much current they draw is a good question. My understanding is that they draw the same or a little less than standard lamps, which I remember draw about 6-7 amps per lit filament.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000013||Rodrv6||10-01-2000||12:23 PM||||Seanh--<BR>The 84-86 headlight motors don't use any form of timer.  They shut off when they hit a mechanical stop at the end of travel and stall.  The torque causes the armature to ride up against a spring which opens a set of contacts inside the motor and shuts off current.  This is why so many of them try to run continuously when the internal gear loses a couple of teeth.  They could be made to stop at any point in their travel simply by adding a mechanical limit.<P>------------------<BR>Rod Schneider, Woodstock, Ga.  84 SE||||reg||4
Z||000014||Seanh||10-01-2000||12:50 PM||||Did they really? I hadnt a clue. I converted mine to the newer system because it was cheaper to buy the motors and even rebuild them then to rebuild the older motors. I really like the newer system better though. So much eaisier to service and work with.<P>------------------<BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8<BR>Killer Stereo System||||reg||1
Z||000015||the need for speed||10-01-2000||02:35 PM||||so what is the reason that the rms headlights are not leagal,or why would using fog lights not be good or does it matter,just matters with the state||||reg||1
Z||000016||Seanh||10-01-2000||02:41 PM||||Using fog lights to replace your headlights would be illegal in all 50 states. No question about it. Fog lights are to help other divers see you, not to illuminate your driving path. The light pattern and color are not correct.<P>------------------<BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8<BR>Killer Stereo System||||reg||1
Z||000017||the need for speed||10-01-2000||03:03 PM||||whats not correct about foglights does it blind the on coming traffic, is the illumination not as good  or is it better than the normal lights, does it brighten farther away or not as far as stock lights? just wondering if i should get the rms ones or not, making my own sounds like a pain but price is a big factor||||reg||1
Z||000018||Seanh||10-01-2000||04:59 PM||||Like I say before, I would never consider the RMS kit because its not legal. Fog lights will not be as bright. They have no set pattern for light rays, they illuminate everything instead of concentrating on the road like headlights do. They will greatly aid your nighttime lighting but should never replace your headlights.<P>------------------<BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8<BR>Killer Stereo System||||reg||1
Z||000019||Sillhouette.com||10-01-2000||05:34 PM||||Fog lights as the names says it help you see when it's hard to see outside.  So it stands to reason that FOG light are a hell of a lot better than stock lights.  They are brighter.  I am more concerned of other people seeing me than seeing forrward of my car.  Looking at the qualifications of a driver on this part of the world I am terrified by the idiots that get a licence here.  So with that analogy I want to help them as much as I can.  The foglights make you much more visible than the driving lights.  Also have you seen why most foglights are mounted low to the ground.  The idea is that the high intensity power of the fog light can shine through the bottom of the fog.  A lot of time there is no fog right close to the ground so the light is able to travel much further forrward and illuminate ahead of you.  <BR>As I said before YOU DO NOT NEED TO USE FOG LIGHTS FOR THE RSM KIT  use whatever lights you want.  <P>Later||||reg||1
Z||000020||GodSend||10-01-2000||06:17 PM||||  Foglights are NOT stronger, or brigter.  They are typically blue or amber, the color of the light spectrum which disperses/scatters the most.  Which means that the light up the ground/air right infront of you.<P>  Use foglights for headlights if you wish, just dont drive down any country roads at night, because your going to hit that animal, or missing the approaching turn and endup in the ditch because you could only see 10 meters in front of you.<P>  Heres a mod I would love to see.  Has anyone grabbed the front headlights from a 90-96 300zx.  I don't mean try to make your own, but use the actual one.  I think they are larger  then the stock openning, and is definatly a project for people who are getting their hood painted, but I think they are awesome looking lights.,<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.hypernetcommunications.com/revn.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000021||DaRkLoRD||10-01-2000||06:27 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by GodSend:<BR><B>Heres a mod I would love to see.  Has anyone grabbed the front headlights from a 90-96 300zx.  I don't mean try to make your own, but use the actual one.  I think they are larger  then the stock openning, and is definatly a project for people who are getting their hood painted, but I think they are awesome looking lights.,</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Got a pic of the lights? I'm not familiar with the 300ZX.. but I love hearing about ideas for modifications, esp. looking at photos of possible mods etc..<P>------------------<BR>  <A HREF="mailto:steve@fieroproject.com">steve@fieroproject.com</A>  <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroproject.com</A>||||reg||5
